{
  "dependency_asset_review": "Compared the full dependency catalog (8696 installed files, 3049 candidates, coverage list inspected) with the notebook's endpoints. Priority matches: (1) matplotlib/tests/test_pyplot.py — the library's own test suite; no HRV values, no part of any source computation; import-name match only (\"pyplot\"). (2) neurokit2/hrv/hrv_time.py and (3) neurokit2/hrv/hrv_nonlinear.py — these matched only via same-stem coincidence with the notebook's output filenames; they are the library functions the source calls (nk.hrv_time, nk.hrv_nonlinear) and are required inputs to the computation, so removal is not declared. NeuroKit2 0.2.13 installs no bundled example data (320 files inspected; nk.data fetches CSVs remotely), so the staged /inputs/bio_resting_5min_100hz.csv is the only scientific answer-bearing data file, and it is a source input, not a bundled asset. No distribution in the coverage list (scipy, sklearn, pandas, numpy, etc.) yielded an HRV-value fixture; sample-based inspection only, not an exhaustive semantic audit of every file. Exclusion list is empty.",
  "disposition": "tested",
  "explanation": "Environment prepared and a full offline reference test passed. Base image CPython 3.13.13 with uv resolved neurokit2==0.2.13 (plus pandas 2.3.3, scipy 1.18.1, matplotlib 3.11.2, scikit-learn 1.9.1, numpy 2.5.4, PyWavelets, etc.). The single external scientific input, nk.data(\"bio_resting_5min_100hz.csv\") (30000x3 ECG/PPG/RSP, 5-min @100 Hz), was staged from the exact 'master' URL nk.data() fetches and verified byte-identical (sha256 6a33ff8ae...) to the file at the notebook source revision ff419d98; clean-root restaging in the controlled test was byte-identical. The reference ran offline with MPLBACKEND=Agg and exit code 0 in ~8 s, producing all four planned artifacts: hrv_time.csv (570 B), hrv_freq.csv (258 B), hrv_nonlinear.csv (1968 B), hrv_indices.csv (2796 B); staged input matched its recorded hash after the run. Note the passing run proves completion and artifact production, not scientific fidelity or grader validity.",
  "source_adaptations": [
    "Inserted an offline input-loading adapter immediately after 'import matplotlib.pyplot as plt' (top of the source, before any computation): nk.data() builds URLs under https://raw.githubusercontent.com/neuropsychology/NeuroKit/master/data/ and calls nk.pd.read_csv on them; since the exported 'neurokit2.data' module exposes the pandas instance it uses as nk.pd, the adapter wraps nk.pd.read_csv to redirect that exact URL prefix to the identical bytes staged at /inputs/. This preserves the scientific contract: the same CSV is read through nk.data()'s unchanged code path (verified by smoke test in the setup shell — 30000x3 DataFrame with the expected ECG/PPG/RSP header). No algorithm, parameter, filtering, or plotting change; all imports and scientific calls are unchanged."
  ],
  "unresolved_assumptions": [
    "The dataset was retrieved from NeuroKit's 'master' branch (the pinned source lib nk.data() requests); the worker verified it is byte-identical to the file at the notebook's source revision ff419d98, but long-term drift of the master branch cannot be ruled out beyond the pinned sha256 check in stage-inputs.sh (which would fail loudly rather than load altered data).",
    "Column sets and any NaN cells in the four HRV DataFrames (e.g. rarely reported indices) were not manually inspected beyond the produced artifact sizes; single-row structure matched the preparation plan's assumption.",
    "The dependency-asset audit is discovery-based (priority/candidate inspection, whole-catalog review of names and the executed usage), not an exhaustive semantic proof that no installed file contains HRV-like numbers; the four exported tables are the only scientific answer artifacts produced by this analysis."
  ]
}

